  ],
  "correct": "C",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Solve each factor separately",
      "body": "<p>The first factor already gives one root. For the quadratic factor, use its discriminant to decide whether it adds zero, one or two real roots.</p>",
      "recap": "A product is zero when at least one factor is zero.",
      "pitfall": "Roots shared by the factors count only once.",
      "pause": "Write the quadratic discriminant and list its three sign cases.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b3-l08",
          "reminder": "A product is zero when at least one factor is zero."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Check when roots coincide",
      "body": "<p>Check two possibilities: the quadratic has a repeated root, or it has two roots and one is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. To test the second possibility, substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> into the quadratic.</p>",
      "recap": "A repeated root is one value, even if it occurs in two factors.",
      "pitfall": "Finding a repeated quadratic root is not the only way to get two different roots.",
      "pause": "Solve both cases, then check the roots in the original product.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b3-l05",
          "reminder": "A repeated root is one value, even if it occurs in two factors."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The root <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> is always present. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, the quadratic discriminant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> is negative, giving only one real root overall.</p><p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, the quadratic is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Its root <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> differs from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so there are exactly two distinct roots.</p><p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, the quadratic has two distinct real roots. There are exactly two overall only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> coincides with one of them. Substituting gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. In this case the roots are 0 and 1.</p><p>Exactly two parameter values work: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. <strong>Answer C: 2.</strong></p>",
